{"name":"Hacienda Blue","hex":"#0087a8","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"hacienda","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A large homestead in a ranch or estate, usually in places where Colonial Spanish culture has had architectural influence."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/hacienda"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Hacienda","blocks":[{"text":"A hacienda is an estate, similar to a Roman latifundium, in Spain and the former Spanish Empire. With origins in Andalusia, haciendas were variously plantations, mines or factories, with many haciendas combining these activities. The word is derived from Spanish hacer and haciendo (making), referring to productive business enterprises."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/71/Hacienda_Lealtad%2C_former_coffee_plantation_in_Lares%2C_Puerto_Rico_03.jpg/330px-Hacienda_Lealtad%2C_former_coffee_plantation_in_Lares%2C_Puerto_Rico_03.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hacienda","wikidata":"Q917730"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1e/Hacienda_of_Xcanchakan%281843%29.jpg/500px-Hacienda_of_Xcanchakan%281843%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1e/Hacienda_of_Xcanchakan%281843%29.jpg/500px-Hacienda_of_Xcanchakan%281843%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Hacienda of Xcanchakan, Yucatán, Mexico","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHacienda_of_Xcanchakan(1843).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/82/Teatro_de_Atequiza%2C_Mexico.JPG/500px-Teatro_de_Atequiza%2C_Mexico.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/82/Teatro_de_Atequiza%2C_Mexico.JPG/500px-Teatro_de_Atequiza%2C_Mexico.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Wheat mill and theatre of Vicente Gallardo; Hacienda Atequiza, Jalisco, Mexico, 1886.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATeatro_de_Atequiza%2C_Mexico.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6d/Hacendado_Propri%C3%A9taire_by_Claudio_Linati_1828.jpg/500px-Hacendado_Propri%C3%A9taire_by_Claudio_Linati_1828.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6d/Hacendado_Propri%C3%A9taire_by_Claudio_Linati_1828.j